 const rows=[
 {pdfPage:17,printedPage:'76',title:'Hydrocarbons and alkanes',text:'Hydrocarbons are the simplest organic compounds and contain only carbon and hydrogen; they are widely used as fuels (diesel, gasoline, natural gas and LPG). They are non-polar covalent compounds. Saturated hydrocarbons are alkanes; unsaturated hydrocarbons are alkenes and alkynes. Alkanes have only single bonds and are saturated; open-chain alkanes have formula CnH2n+2. Ring alkanes are cycloalkanes with formula CnH2n. They are relatively less reactive (paraffins) and form a homologous series.'},
 {pdfPage:18,printedPage:'77',title:'First five alkanes: formulae and structures',text:'The source table gives methane CH4, ethane C2H6 (condensed CH3CH3), propane C3H8 (CH3CH2CH3), butane C4H10 (CH3(CH2)2CH3), pentane C5H12 (CH3(CH2)3CH3), with molecular, condensed, structural and electron dot/cross structures. The displayed covalent diagrams show carbon sharing electron pairs with hydrogen and neighbouring carbon atoms.'},
 {pdfPage:19,printedPage:'78',title:'IUPAC nomenclature: parent chain and numbering',text:'The source notes that before 1947 organic naming used a common system; IUPAC established an international systematic standard so each different compound has a distinct name. Alkane naming begins by selecting the longest continuous carbon chain (parent chain) with maximum branches. Number the parent from the end that gives substituents the lowest positions. The parent name reflects carbon count (e.g. pentane, heptane). A reading check compares structural formulae of propane and cyclopropane.'},
 {pdfPage:20,printedPage:'79',title:'IUPAC nomenclature: substituent positions and multiple branches',text:'After choosing and numbering the parent chain, place its name last and prefix each branch/substituent with its position number (e.g. 2-methylbutane, 2-methylhexane, 2-methylpropane). When multiple branches occur, state each substituent position (2,4-dimethylhexane; 2,3,5-trimethylhexane). If two branches attach to the same carbon, repeat that locant (2,2,5-trimethylhexane; 2,2,4-trimethylhexane).'}
 ];
