 {pdfPage:5,printedPage:null,title:'Unit 14 Environmental Chemistry: Atmosphere learning outcomes',text:'The opener names Unit 14 Environmental Chemistry: The Atmosphere and lists outcomes to define atmosphere; explain atmospheric composition; describe atmosphere layers; summarize pollutant sources/effects; describe acid rain and its effects; explain ozone depletion; and describe global warming. The scan includes a diagram naming troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere and exosphere.'},
 {pdfPage:6,printedPage:'127',title:'Environmental chemistry and atmosphere introduction',text:'Earth formed about 4.5 billion years ago and early conditions could not sustain life. Cooling/condensation of steam and atmospheric gases/stratospheric ozone made Earth suitable. Environmental chemistry studies the environment and changes within it. Earth has geosphere/lithosphere, hydrosphere, atmosphere and biosphere; environmental chemistry studies these natural systems and changes. Atmosphere is the protective blanket of gases around Earth, supports life and shields against cosmic and UV radiation. Major gases N2/O2; minor CO2/Ar and traces. Density, temperature and pressure change with altitude.'},
 {pdfPage:7,printedPage:'128',title:'Air and composition of the atmosphere',text:'Atmospheric gases used in respiration/photosynthesis are called air; people breathe O2 and exhale CO2, plants use CO2 in food preparation, and fewer plants can change atmospheric CO2 and contribute to pollution. Composition varies by time/place and includes gases, water vapour and dust. Dry air by volume: nitrogen 78.1%, oxygen 20.9% (about 99% together); argon 0.9%, CO2 0.03%, hydrogen 0.01%, neon 0.0018%, helium 0.0005%, ozone 0.00006%, other gases 0.00006% (source table); water vapour 0–4%. The book states atmospheric mass about 4.5–5×10^15 metric tonnes and density decreases with altitude.'},
 {pdfPage:8,printedPage:null,title:'Atmospheric layers and troposphere',text:'The diagram shows approximate layer boundaries: troposphere to 12 km, stratosphere to 50 km, mesosphere to 80 km, thermosphere to 320 km, with exosphere above. Atmospheric layers vary with temperature; sea-level pressure is about 1 atm and pressure decreases with altitude. The book focuses on troposphere and stratosphere. Troposphere is the lowest layer, where weather conditions vary; average height about 12 km and varies with latitude, seasons and pressure.'}
