 const p21={pdfPage:21,printedPage:'19',title:'Unit 9 exercise: MCQs 7–10 and short questions 1–6',text:'MCQs 7–10 ask what a very large Kc indicates; define active mass; infer the balanced reaction from Kc=[C]^4/([A]^3[B]^2); and find Kc units for PCl3(g)+Cl2(g) ⇌ PCl5(g). The short questions ask to define chemical equilibrium with two examples; identify dynamic equilibrium; compare macroscopic characteristics of forward and reverse reactions; state information needed to predict reaction direction; relate active mass to reaction rate; and calculate Kc at 500°C for N2/H2/NH3 concentrations 0.602/0.420/0.113 mol dm−3 (printed answer 0.286 mol−2 dm6).'};
 const p22={pdfPage:22,printedPage:'20',title:'Unit 9 exercise: remaining short and long questions; project',text:'Short questions 7–10 ask for necessary equilibrium conditions; Kc expressions for N2+3H2 ⇌ 2NH3, 2H2+O2 ⇌ 2H2O and 4NH3+5O2 ⇌ 4NO+6H2O; calculate Kc for SO2/O2/SO3 at 600°C from concentrations 1.50/1.25/3.50 mol dm−3 (printed answer 4.36 mol−1 dm3); and describe temperature’s effect on Kc. Long questions: (1) 2SO3 decomposition, chemical equation, Kc expression and units; (2) describe equilibrium with graph/example and define Law of Mass Action; (3) derive Kc and units and predict direction from Kc; (4) show Kc has different units with examples and predict reaction extent; (5) for Kc=[Cl2]^2[H2O]^2/([HCl]^4[O2]), identify reactants/products and derive units, then explain the importance of Kc with examples/reasons. Project asks which factors affect ammonia production: temperature, pressure, concentration and catalyst.'};
 const p23={pdfPage:23,printedPage:'21',title:'Unit 10 learning outcomes',text:'Students Learning Outcomes (All the students will be able to): (1) Define and give examples of Arrhenius acids and bases (Understanding). (2) Use the Bronsted–Lowery theory to classify substances as acids or bases, or as proton donors or proton acceptors (Applying). (3) Classify substances as Lewis acids or bases (Analyzing). (4) Write the equation for the self-ionization of water (Remembering). (5) Given the hydrogen-ion or hydroxide-ion concentration, classify a solution as neutral, acidic or basic (Applying). (6) Complete and balance a neutralization reaction (Applying).'};
 const p24={pdfPage:24,printedPage:'22',title:'Unit 10 introduction and Fig. 10.1 acid sources',text:'The unit introduction says lemons, oranges, grapefruits and other citrus fruits taste sour because citric acid is present. Bases (alkalis) have a bitter taste and feel soapy; soap and toothpaste are examples. Salts are formed by an acid-base reaction; table salt and baking products are common salts. The unit covers concepts of acids and bases, pH, neutralization reactions, salts and their types. Fig. 10.1 maps acids to sources: carbonic acid—carbonated drinks; lactic acid—sour milk; malic acid—apples and pears; citric acid—limes and lemons; formic acid—insects; oxalic acid—spinach; tartaric acid—grape juice. The real-world reading says sour milk contains lactic acid, apples contain malic acid, grape juice contains tartaric acid, ants produce formic acid, and bee stings are acidic due to formic acid. Household products: ammonia (NH3) is used for cleaning; sodium hydroxide (NaOH), commonly called lye, is used as a drain cleaner and for cleaning ovens; milk of magnesia is a suspension in water of magnesium hydroxide Mg(OH)2; magnesium hydroxide and aluminium hydroxide Al(OH)3 are used as antacids to relieve discomfort caused by excess hydrochloric acid in the stomach. The text concludes these are bases.'};
