 const p=[
 {pdfPage:25,printedPage:'23',title:'Properties of acids and bases; alkalis',text:'Fig. 10.2 labels ammonia, sodium hydroxide and baking soda. Topic 10.1: “acidus” is Latin for sour. Acids taste sour, turn blue litmus paper red, change acid-base indicator colours, conduct electricity in aqueous solution and strong acids are corrosive, destroying fabric and causing skin burns. A base is any metal oxide or hydroxide that reacts with an acid to produce salt and water. A water-soluble base is an alkali; in aqueous solution it produces OH− ions. All alkalis are bases, but all bases are not alkalis. Fig. 10.4 shows alkalis as a subset within bases. Bases taste bitter, feel soapy, turn red litmus paper blue, change indicator colours, and strong bases are corrosive and conduct electricity when dissolved in water. Fig. 10.3 shows pH paper turning red in vinegar; Fig. 10.5 shows pH paper turning blue in sodium hydroxide solution.'},
 {pdfPage:26,printedPage:'24',title:'Activity 10.1 and acid ionization',text:'Activity 10.1: Reaction of Lemon Juice (acid) with Baking Soda. You will need a balloon, a soft drink bottle, lemon juice and baking soda. Instructions: (1) Before you start, stretch out the balloon to make it as easy as possible to inflate. (2) Pour lemon juice into the soft drink bottle. (3) Add baking soda into the balloon. (4) Put the balloon over the mouth of the bottle and observe what happened. Write your own observation and the reaction. Section 10.1.1 states Svante Arrhenius, a Swedish chemist, explained that aqueous solutions of acids and bases conduct electric current by producing ions; according to the Arrhenius concept (1884), an acid dissociates in aqueous solution to give H+ ions. General ionization: HA(aq) ⇌ H+(aq) + A−(aq). Examples named: HCl, H2SO4, HNO3, CH3COOH and HCN. Table 10.1 “Few Acids and Their Ionization”: hydrochloric acid HCl → H+(aq)+Cl−(aq); sulphuric acid H2SO4 → H+(aq)+HSO4−(aq); nitric acid HNO3 → H+(aq)+NO3−(aq); acetic acid CH3COOH ⇌ CH3COO−(aq)+H+(aq); phosphoric acid H3PO4 ⇌ 3H+(aq)+PO4^3−(aq).'},
 {pdfPage:27,printedPage:'25',title:'Arrhenius bases, ionization table, limitations and Example 10.1',text:'An Arrhenius base dissociates in aqueous solution to give hydroxide ions OH−. Most bases dissociate to release OH−; other bases react with water to remove a hydrogen ion, leaving hydroxide ions in solution. General ionization: BOH(aq) ⇌ B+(aq)+OH−(aq). Examples named: NaOH, KOH, NH4OH, Mg(OH)2, Ca(OH)2 and Al(OH)3. Table 10.2 “Few acids and their ionization” lists: sodium hydroxide NaOH → Na+(aq)+OH−(aq); potassium hydroxide KOH → K+(aq)+OH−(aq); ammonium hydroxide NH4OH ⇌ NH4+(aq)+OH−(aq); magnesium hydroxide Mg(OH)2 ⇌ Mg2+(aq)+2OH−(aq); calcium hydroxide Ca(OH)2 ⇌ Ca2+(aq)+2OH−(aq); aluminium hydroxide Al(OH)3 ⇌ Al3+(aq)+3OH−(aq). Summary: acids give H+ in water, bases give OH− in water. Limitations: (i) Arrhenius is limited to aqueous medium and does not explain acidity or basicity in non-aqueous media; (ii) acids are limited to H+ and bases to OH−, so it cannot explain acidic CO2 or basic NH3. Example 10.1 asks which of (a) Ca(OH)2, (b) HNO3, (c) NaOH, (d) H2SO4 and (e) KOH are Arrhenius acids and bases. Printed solution: (i) (b) and (d) are acids because they donate hydrogen ions in aqueous solution; (ii) (a), (c) and (e) are bases because they donate hydroxide ions in aqueous solution.'},
 {pdfPage:28,printedPage:'26',title:'Arrhenius self-assessment and Brønsted–Lowry concept opening',text:'Self Assessment asks: (1) Define Arrhenius acid and base. (2) Write chemical formulae and names of three Arrhenius acids and bases. (3) What are the limitations of the Arrhenius concept? Section 10.1.2: In 1923 Danish chemist J. N. Brønsted and English chemist T. M. Lowry independently expanded the Arrhenius definitions. A Brønsted–Lowry acid is a substance (molecule or ion) that donates a proton (H+); a base is a substance (molecule or ion) that accepts a proton (H+). For HCl(aq)+NH3(aq) ⇌ NH4+(aq)+Cl−(aq), HCl donates a proton and is acid; ammonia accepts it and is base. Water acts as a Brønsted–Lowry acid when donating a proton to ammonia: H2O+NH3 ⇌ NH4++OH−. Water acts as a base when accepting a proton from HCl: H2O+HCl → H3O++Cl−. Thus water can act as acid and base (amphoteric). Fig. 10.6 caption: Hydrochloric acid gas reacts with ammonia gas resulting in smoke of solid ammonium chloride.'}
 ];
